CAT = "cfpTax"; SEG = "TAX"
# E. Tax Planning (E.36-E.43). Rules that do not change yearly; computed keys asserted.
eff = 14300 / 92000; assert round(eff * 100, 1) == 15.5
credit_vs_ded = 1000 - 1000 * 0.24; assert credit_vs_ded == 760
gain_home = 742000 - 316000 - 500000; assert gain_home < 0
gift_gain = 31000 - 12000; assert gift_gain == 19000
inst = (90000 - 36000) / 90000; assert round(inst, 2) == 0.60 and 20000 * inst == 12000
stock_ded = 0.30 * 150000; assert stock_ded == 45000
harvest = 18000 - 21000; assert harvest == -3000
Q = [
("A client owes $14,300 of tax on $92,000 of taxable income and her top bracket is 22%. Her average tax rate is about:",
 "15.5%", ["22.0%", "14.3%", "18.6%"],
 "The average (effective) rate is total tax over taxable income: 14,300 / 92,000 = 15.5%. The 22% bracket is her marginal rate."),
("For a taxpayer in the 24% bracket, how much more is a $1,000 credit worth than a $1,000 deduction?",
 "$760", ["$240", "$1,000", "$0"],
 "A credit cuts tax by $1,000; a deduction cuts it by 24% x $1,000 = $240. The difference is $760."),
("Which IRS guidance can be relied on only by the taxpayer who asked for it?",
 "Private letter ruling", ["Revenue ruling", "Treasury regulation", "Revenue procedure"],
 "A private letter ruling answers one taxpayer's request and binds the IRS only for that taxpayer. Revenue rulings and regulations apply to everyone in the same facts."),
("Two owners want pass-through taxation, limited liability and freedom to split profits unevenly. The best fit is:",
 "An LLC taxed as a partnership", ["An S corporation", "A C corporation", "A general partnership"],
 "A multi-member LLC taxed as a partnership can make special allocations with substance. An S corporation must allocate pro rata by shares, and a general partnership gives no liability shield."),
("Which change would end a company's S corporation election?",
 "Issuing a second class of stock", ["Adding a 70th shareholder", "Admitting a resident alien", "Paying a reasonable salary"],
 "IRC section 1361 allows only one class of stock, at most 100 shareholders and no nonresident alien owners. Differences in voting rights alone do not create a second class."),
("A sole proprietor reports business income and expenses on:",
 "Schedule C", ["Form 1065", "Form 1120-S", "Schedule E"],
 "A sole proprietorship is not a separate taxpayer: its profit or loss goes on Schedule C of the owner's Form 1040. Form 1065 is the partnership return."),
("Which retained power makes an irrevocable trust a grantor trust for income tax?",
 "Swapping assets of equal value", ["Naming an independent trustee", "A spendthrift clause", "Paying income to a charity"],
 "IRC section 675 treats the grantor as owner when he can reacquire trust assets by substituting property of equal value. Such an intentionally defective trust stays out of his estate."),
("Why does a complex trust that keeps its income often pay more tax than an individual would?",
 "Its brackets are compressed", ["It has no standard deduction rules", "It pays payroll taxes", "It cannot deduct expenses"],
 "Trust tax brackets reach the top rate at a far lower income than individual brackets, so income kept in a trust is taxed heavily. Distributing it shifts tax to beneficiaries."),
("A complex trust has $22,000 of distributable net income and distributes $30,000. The beneficiaries are taxed on:",
 "$22,000", ["$30,000", "$8,000", "$52,000"],
 "Distributable net income caps the amount taxed to beneficiaries, so they report $22,000. The extra $8,000 is treated as a tax-free distribution of principal."),
("Which account is usually the best place to hold high-yield taxable bonds?",
 "A traditional IRA", ["A taxable brokerage account", "A custodial UTMA account", "A joint account"],
 "Interest is taxed as ordinary income each year, so holding it in a tax-deferred account shelters it. Assets with qualified dividends or growth fit taxable accounts better."),
("A client has $21,000 of losses and $18,000 of gains from sales in the same year. The net is:",
 "A $3,000 capital loss", ["A $3,000 capital gain", "A $39,000 capital loss", "Zero, losses are lost"],
 "Gains and losses are netted: 18,000 - 21,000 = a $3,000 net capital loss, which can offset ordinary income up to the annual limit."),
("A married couple sells the home they lived in for 9 years for $742,000. Their basis is $316,000. The taxable gain is:",
 "$0", ["$426,000", "$176,000", "$242,000"],
 "The gain is 742,000 - 316,000 = $426,000, under the $500,000 joint exclusion for a main home owned and used 2 of the last 5 years, so nothing is taxed."),
("Ben received stock as a gift with a donor basis of $12,000 and a value of $15,000. He sells it for $31,000. His gain is:",
 "$19,000", ["$16,000", "$31,000", "$15,000"],
 "When value at the gift is above the donor's basis, the donee takes the donor's basis: 31,000 - 12,000 = $19,000. Only a loss sale uses the lower value at the gift."),
("Lia inherits land her father bought for $40,000 that is worth $210,000 at his death. Her basis is generally:",
 "$210,000", ["$40,000", "$125,000", "$170,000"],
 "Property acquired from a decedent takes a basis equal to its fair market value at death, $210,000, a step-up that wipes out the $170,000 of gain."),
("A seller of land with a $36,000 basis receives $20,000 of principal this year on a $90,000 installment sale. The gain reported this year is:",
 "$12,000", ["$20,000", "$54,000", "$8,000"],
 "Gross profit ratio = (90,000 - 36,000) / 90,000 = 60%. Gain reported = 60% x $20,000 of principal = $12,000."),
("Since 2018, a tax-deferred like-kind exchange under section 1031 is available only for:",
 "Real property", ["Any business property", "Stocks and bonds", "Personal residences"],
 "The 2017 tax act limited section 1031 to real property held for business or investment. Equipment and vehicles no longer qualify, and a main home never did."),
("A former wife receives part of her ex-husband's 401(k) under a QDRO and takes it in cash at age 45. She owes:",
 "Regular tax, no penalty", ["Regular tax plus 10%", "Nothing at all", "Tax only on the gain"],
 "A distribution to an alternate payee under a qualified domestic relations order is exempt from the 10% additional tax, but it is still ordinary income to her."),
("Shares held exactly one year and one day before sale produce a gain that is:",
 "Long-term", ["Short-term", "Ordinary income", "Always tax-free"],
 "A holding period of more than one year makes a capital gain long-term. Exactly one year would still be short-term."),
("A client with $150,000 of AGI gives long-term appreciated stock to a public charity. His deduction for the year is limited to:",
 "$45,000", ["$75,000", "$90,000", "$30,000"],
 "Gifts of long-term capital gain property to a public charity, deducted at fair market value, are limited to 30% of AGI: 30% x $150,000 = $45,000. Any excess carries forward five years."),
("Giving appreciated long-term stock to charity instead of selling it first and giving cash lets the donor:",
 "Avoid tax on the gain", ["Deduct twice the value", "Deduct only the basis", "Defer tax to a later year"],
 "The donor deducts fair market value and never recognizes the gain. Selling first would trigger capital gain tax before the gift."),
("A client gives to charity every other year in larger amounts so she can itemize in those years. This is called:",
 "Bunching", ["Harvesting", "Netting", "Stacking"],
 "Bunching concentrates two years of gifts into one so itemized deductions top the standard deduction, then takes the standard deduction in the off year."),
("A client puts appreciated stock into a donor-advised fund this year and recommends grants over later years. When is the deduction taken?",
 "In the year of the gift", ["When grants are made", "Spread over five years", "Only at death"],
 "A contribution to a donor-advised fund is a completed gift to the sponsoring charity, so the deduction comes in the year of transfer."),
("A client receives a 1099-K for $8,200 from selling household furniture at a loss. The sale is:",
 "A nondeductible personal loss", ["A deductible capital loss", "Ordinary business income", "Taxable at its full amount"],
 "Losses on personal-use property are not deductible. Only gains on personal property are taxed."),
("Which item increases a taxpayer's alternative minimum taxable income?",
 "Bargain element of exercised ISOs", ["Qualified Roth IRA withdrawals in retirement", "Interest on a school bond", "Qualified dividends"],
 "Exercising an incentive stock option and keeping the shares adds the spread to AMT income though it is not regular income. Qualified dividends keep their rates under AMT."),
("A nonqualified stock option is exercised when the stock is $50 and the strike is $30. The $20 spread per share is:",
 "Ordinary compensation income", ["A long-term capital gain", "Tax-free until sale", "An AMT item only"],
 "At exercise of a nonqualified option the spread is wages. The basis becomes the market price, and later growth is capital gain."),
("A real estate professional who materially participates in her rentals treats their losses as:",
 "Nonpassive", ["Passive", "Capital losses", "Nondeductible"],
 "Under IRC section 469 a qualifying real estate professional's rental activities in which she materially participates are not passive, so losses offset other income."),
("A self-employed client can deduct which part of the self-employment tax?",
 "The employer-equivalent half", ["All of it", "None of it", "Only the Medicare part"],
 "Half of self-employment tax, the employer's share, is an above-the-line deduction in figuring AGI."),
("An S corporation owner-employee pays himself no salary and takes all profit as distributions. The risk is that the IRS:",
 "Recasts part as wages", ["Revokes the S election", "Taxes the distributions twice", "Disallows the business"],
 "Shareholder-employees must receive reasonable compensation for services. The IRS can recharacterize distributions as wages subject to payroll taxes."),
]
